ains the coordinates of ${S}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${S}^{′}(14,-7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281085" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-281085" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281085" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281085" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281085" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${F}^{′}$, the image of $F(2,-2)$ under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $F$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $F:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${F}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${F}^{′}(2,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-2,6)"] ["math" "(1,6)"] ["math" "(2,-4)"] ["math" "(2,6)"] ["math" "(6,-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(2,6)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-81.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-3$ passes through the points $A(0,-3)$ and $B(3,-5)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-3,-6)$ and ${B}^{′}(7,-13).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{-13+6}{7-(-3)}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =-\\frac{7}{10}(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10y+60 & =-7x-21 \\\\ 7x+10y & =-81\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281130" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7x+10y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=2x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=2x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-1,1)$ and ${B}^{′}(0,5).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{5-1}{0+1}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 4x-y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-281241" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281241" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x-y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=x+3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=x+3$ passes through the points $C(0,3)$ and $L(1,4)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $C$ and $L$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${C}^{′}(-3,3)$ and ${L}^{′}(0,5).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${C}^{′}$ and ${L}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5-3}{0-(-3)}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ 3y-9 & =2x+6 \\\\ 2x-3y+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-77781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+3y-15=0"] ["math" "2x-3y+15=0"] ["math" "2x-3y+5=0"] ["math" "3x+2y-5=0"] ["math" "3x-2y+15=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x-3y+15=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=4x+5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=4x+5$ passes through the points $G(0,5)$ and $H(1,9)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $G$ and $H$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -15 & -28\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${G}^{′}(0,-15)$ and ${H}^{′}(2,-28).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${G}^{′}$ and ${H}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-15) & =\\frac{-28-(-15)}{2-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+15 & =-\\frac{13}{2}x \\\\ 2y+30 & =-13x \\\\ 13x+2y+30 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77770" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-77770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2x+y-15=0"] ["math" "12x+y+5=0"] ["math" "13x+2y+30=0"] ["math" "2x+y+5=0"] ["math" "4x-y-5=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77770" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "13x+2y+30=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=-x-4$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=-x-4$ passes through the points $W(0,-4)$ and $Z(1,-5)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $W$ and $Z$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${W}^{′}(8,0)$ and ${Z}^{′}(9,-3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${W}^{′}$ and ${Z}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{-3-0}{9-8}(x-8) \\\\ y & =-3(x-8) \\\\ y & =-3x+24 \\\\ 3x+y-24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3x+y+24=0"] ["math" "3x+y-24=0"] ["math" "3x+y-8=0"] ["math" "3x-y+24=0"] ["math" "3x-y+8=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x+y-24=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $4x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=27.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $4x-y-3=0$ passes through the points $U(0,-3)$ and $V(1,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $U$ and $V$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 5 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${U}^{′}(-12,-3)$ and ${V}^{′}(5,-1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =\\frac{-1-(-3)}{5-(-12)}(x-(-12)) \\\\ y+3 & =\\frac{2}{17}(x+12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{2}{17}(x+12) \\\\ 17y+51 & =2x+24 \\\\ 51-24 & =2x-17y \\\\ 2x-17y & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-275719" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x-17y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $2x+y-3=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $2x+y-3=0$ passes through the points $A(0,3)$ and $B(1,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(0,6)$ and ${B}^{′}(-2,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-6 & =\\frac{3-6}{-2-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-6 & =\\frac{3}{2}x \\\\ 2y-12 & =3x \\\\ 3x-2y+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77653" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-77653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x+3y-6=0"] ["math" "2x+y-3=0"] ["math" "2x-y+12=0"] ["math" "3x-2y+12=0"] ["math" "x+2y-3=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-77653" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3x-2y+12=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-275715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $x=-2$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-275715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $x=-2$ passes through the points $G(-2,0)$ and $H(-2,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $G$ and $H$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${G}^{′}(2,-6)$ and ${H}^{′}(6,-4).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{-4-(-6)}{6-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ 2y+12 & =x-2 \\\\ 12+2 & =x-2y \\\\ x-2y & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-275715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-275715" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-275715" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $-4x+y+1=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $-4x+y+1=0$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(-1,-2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(6,6).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6-(-2)}{6-(-1)}(x+1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{8}{7}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y+14 & =8x+8 \\\\ 8x-7y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x-7y=6\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-281586" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281586" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8x-7y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $3x+2y-6=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=42.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $3x+2y-6=0$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(2,0)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,-6)$ and ${Q}^{′}(4,2).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{2-(-6)}{4-9}(x-9) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{8}{-5}(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+6 & =-\\frac{8}{5}(x-9) \\\\ 5y+30 & =-8x+72 \\\\ 8x+5y & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x+5y=42\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "q-281583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8x+5y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "29466f38154780dd93683e3031f3c00c5fe3fef3fc8fb3b59adb43e2de760daa"} {"question" "e-6704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B',$ the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6704.json" "file_sha256" "290cbab794266e393eb563de4b050cc13ebaab2aba4ccd812216c79830af05ec"} {"question" "e-6704" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x},$ contains the coordinates of $B'.$ Therefore, $B'(3,-5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6704.json" "file_sha256" "290cbab794266e393eb563de4b050cc13ebaab2aba4ccd812216c79830af05ec"} {"question" "e-6713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6713.json" "file_sha256" "839860b0f95518b40d5286d9ea0def1513b90db98e96e6374d67f61bd9f8873f"} {"question" "e-6713" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=3(0)-2=-2, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=3(1)-2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-0}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =-6x \\\\ 6x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6713.json" "file_sha256" "839860b0f95518b40d5286d9ea0def1513b90db98e96e6374d67f61bd9f8873f"} {"question" "e-6705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6705.json" "file_sha256" "a5e9bd7dc476bdce501d3b2b13d35ca962969e96a980da0a7cba3de685886420"} {"question" "e-6705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y,$ we get $y=5x-3,$ and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0\\, & \\to \\,y=5(0)-3=-3, \\\\ x=1\\, & \\to \\,y=5(1)-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2).$\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-6) & =\\frac{3-(-6)}{-1-(-3)}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{9}{2}(x+3) \\\\ 2y+12 & =9x+27 \\\\ 9x-2y+15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/example-6705.json" "file_sha256" "a5e9bd7dc476bdce501d3b2b13d35ca962969e96a980da0a7cba3de685886420"}] "usage" {"e-6705" [] "e-6704" [] "q-77543" [] "q-77653" [] "e-6713" []} "basis" 868 "retractions" [[17592186152692 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $A^{′}$, the image of $A(-1, 1)$ under the action of $T$?"] [17592186152693 :question/problem "Consider the line $2x + y - 3 = 0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$?"] [17592186151901 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $B'$, the image of $B(2,-3)$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$?"] [17592186151901 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $B$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a column vector $\\mathbf{x}$ containing the coordinates of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $T\\mathbf{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTx &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $T \\mathbf{x}$, contains the coordinates of $B'$. Therefore, $B'(3,-5)$."] [17592186151903 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=3x-2$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T$?"] [17592186151903 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find two points on the given line. Substituting $x=0$ and $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x = 0 & → y = 3(0) - 2 =-2, \\\\ x = 1 & → y = 3(1) - 2 = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-2)$ and $Q(1,1)$.\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(0,-2)$ and $Q'(-1,4)$.\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}}(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-2) &= (4 - (-2))/(-1 - 0)(x - 0) \\\\\ny + 2 &= -6x \\\\\n6x + y + 2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151902 :question/problem "Consider the line $5x-y-3=0$ and linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $\\mathbf T$?"] [17592186151902 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find two points on the given line. Solving for $y$, we get $y=5x-3$, and substituting $x=0$ and $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x = 0 & → y = 5(0) - 3 =-3, \\\\ x = 1 & → y = 5(1) - 3 = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo we have two points on the line, $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(1,2)$.\n\nTo find the images of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of $P$ and $Q$ in its columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTX &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are $P'(-3,-6)$ and $Q'(-1,3)$.\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through $P'$ and $Q'$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}}(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-6) &= (3 - (-6))/(-1 - (-3))(x - (-3)) \\\\\ny + 6 &= \\frac{9}{2}(x + 3) \\\\\n2y + 12 &= 9x + 27 \\\\\n9x - 2y + 15 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"